sentences.t:
—“Mugier doña Ximena, grado al Criador.
A vos digo, mis fijas, don Elvira e doña Sol:
deste vu[e]stro casamiento creçr[e]mos en onor;

sentences.l:
—“Wife doña Ximena, thanks to-the Creator.
To you I-say, my daughters, don Elvira and doña Sol:
of-this yo[u]r marriage we-shall-gr[o]w in estate;

sentences.t:
Reçibiólos mio Çid con todos s[o]s vasallos;
a el[l]e e a ssu mugier delant se le omillaron,
e ivan posar en un preçioso escaño.

sentences.l:
Received-them my Çid with all h[i]s vassals;
to him and to his wife before themselves to-him they-bowed,
and they-went to-sit on a precious bench.

sentences.t:
“Pues que a fazer lo avemos, por qué lo imos tardando?
Venit acá, Álbar Fáñez, el que yo quiero e amo!
affé amas mis fijas, métolas en vuestra mano;

sentences.l:
“Since that to do it we-have, why it we-go delaying?
Come here, Álbar Fáñez, he whom I hold-dear and love!
behold both my daughters, I-put-them in your hand;

sentences.t:
el obispo don Jero[me] vistiós tan privado,
a la puerta de la eclegia sediellos sperando;
dióles bendictiones, la missa a cantado.

sentences.l:
the bishop don Jero[me] vested-himself so quickly,
at the door of the church he-was-them waiting;
he-gave-them blessings, the mass he-has sung.

sentences.t:
Mio Çid don Rodrigo, el que en buen ora nasco,
entre palafrés e mulas e corredores cavallos,
en bestias sines al çiento [ha] mandados;

sentences.l:
My Çid don Rodrigo, he who in good hour was-born,
between palfreys and mules and running horses,
in beasts without other a-hundred [he-has] given;
